  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:58:07.056Z",
  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nIs a taxpayer who earns both compensation income and business income required to file an annual Income Tax Return (ITR)?\n\n### Short Answer\nYes, a taxpayer earning both compensation income and business income is required to file an annual ITR. This obligation is established under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC).\n\n### Accounting Treatment\nFor accounting purposes, the taxpayer must maintain accurate records of both compensation and business income. This includes documenting all sources of income, expenses related to the business, and any withholding tax on compensation income.\n\n### Tax Treatment\nAccording to Section 51 of the NIRC, individuals deriving income from multiple sources, including both compensation and business income, must file an ITR regardless of the amount of gross income. Specifically, individuals with compensation income from multiple employers or those with business income must report their total taxable income and pay the corresponding income tax.\n\n### Differences and Reconciliation\nThe requirement to file an ITR applies uniformly to individuals with mixed income sources. However, individuals whose sole income is purely compensation and has been subjected to correct withholding tax may not be required to file. In contrast, those with business income or multiple compensation sources must file to ensure compliance with tax obligations.\n\n### Practical Note\nTaxpayers should be aware of their filing deadlines and ensure that they have all necessary documentation ready for the preparation of their ITR. Filing is typically due on or before April 15 of the following year, covering the income earned in the preceding taxable year.",
